A||||11||Eric_PA||ECM Quad Driver Error (CODE 26)||||5||
Z||000000||Eric_PA||12-16-2001||10:17 AM||||Ok, I know this problem is not for a 2.8 or 2.5, it's for my 90 3800 with an 8253 ECM, but it's in my fiero   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> Anyway, I can't figure out what could cause this particular QUAD driver error (code 26) I know it has to be some sort of output/input not being used properly, but I can't figure out which one it is. Does anyone have a clue which one it could be. Also this setup is using a manual trans not the automatic that should be hooked up the the 3800.<P>Have allready tried....<BR>* TCC with resistors, but only used 1K should it be lower Resistance.<BR>* Tied the gear selector inputs to ground (that got rid of codes 27,28,29)<BR>* The purge selonoid is hooked up.<BR>* Power steering pressure tied to ground.<BR>* Brake input to ECM is hooked up.<P>I'm out of ideas.<P>Thanks,<BR>Eric<BR><p>[This message has been edited by Eric_PA (edited 12-16-2001).]||||reg||5
Z||000001||GTDude||12-16-2001||11:01 AM||||Eric, you need to hook it up to a good scanner and find out which module is malfunctioning.  When you find out which module, refer to the car manual of the vehicle you took the engine from.  Without doing this you can keep replacing parts with no results.  It could be something VERY simple.   I'm a GM tech for the past 25 years specializing in electrical and ecm related problems.  If I can help further let me know.<P>Phil Pettus<P>------------------<BR>GTDude's 86GT||||reg||3
Z||000002||pherder||12-16-2001||11:19 AM||||Eric,<P>GTDude has a good point ... finding which module is malfunctioning is the only way to find which is setting the code 26.<P>On my Quad 4 install, I had a code 26 set and it was because the ECM was programmed to turn on the coolant fan (radiator).  If anything else did, it set a code 26.<P>Turns out that the Fiero's A/C switch grounds out the radiator fan relay to turn the fan.  I clipped that wire and the code 26 went away.<P>If I remember correctly, the quad driver is 4 different circuits ... any one of them can set the code including the bulb in the instrument panel being blown or not hooked up.  The Quad 4 ECM sends a signal to light a bulb (SES) for high temp.  I didn't have it hooked up and set a code 26.  Same code would have been set if the bulb was burnt out.<P>Paul||||reg||1
Z||000003||Eric_PA||12-16-2001||12:42 PM||||Thanks for the info guys. I did have a scanner hooked up to it last night (thanks Rockcrawl) but basically it was getting late and tried many things. I am going to check out the AC request thing and the temp bulb, but I do rmember the coolant temp bulb lighting up when I first got it going, It overheated on me due to the coolant sytem not air free yet  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"> I should be getting my scanner in the mail tomorrow, so hopefully I can get to the bottom of it. I must say, the car moves really well until the it goes into closed loop, so this code must be hindering the perfomance.<P>Thakns again,<BR>Eric||||reg||1
Z||000004||theogre||12-16-2001||01:19 PM||||Quad drivers are just that, driver chips capable of running, AKA driving, 4 circuits. If you've got an error, either the quad driver chip is actually bad or the ECM simply thinks it is. The later ECMs have monitoring on QD outputs. If, as indicated above, something is overriding the QD, like the AC controls, then the ECM will think the QD has failed and trip a code. If a load is missing from a circuit the ECM is trying to turn on, you could have the same result.<P>The quick test for AC,,, put the AC controls in vent or off to make sure they aren't activating the fan.<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/fullstaff.gif">  <FONT COLOR="blue"><I><B>11-Sept-01, The day the world as we knew it ended.</B></I></FONT>||||reg||1
Z||000005||terryk||12-16-2001||01:59 PM||||I just looked at my 90 docs and it looks like you have a problem in your TCC fake out. Try TCC brake straight to +12V rather than through a resistor. Also, try dropping the 1k's down to 470's. 1K alway worked for me, but the relays you are simulating are below 100 ohms (20-50). The ECM wants pull ups to make it think the load is there. <P>This code will set when:<P>1. Closed loop<P>2. And one of the following conditions:<BR>  <BR>   A. Brake Switch is ONN and QDM circuit 1,2 or 4 occurs.<P>   B. Brake Switch is off and QDM circuit 1,2,3 or 4 occurs.<P><p>[This message has been edited by terryk (edited 12-16-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000006||rockcrawl||12-16-2001||07:52 PM||||This is how it's hooked up now. A 1.1k resistor takes the place of the TCC. We measured a TCC coil and it's about 22 ohms. We figured at 22 ohms we'd need a 7 watt!! resistor. I wasn't sure about the three gear switch inputs being grounded, can't find any real info about how they worked in the '88 Buick. Eric said when he grounded them he lost codes 27, 28, and 29.<P> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/erics38.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000007||terryk||12-16-2001||09:06 PM||||By any chance does your manual show a how lamp too? This is on Quad A output also in the later 1470 ECM's and the 8253 is just an earlier version.<P>I'm looking through my docs now.<P>That wiring looks fine so I suspect there is another output that's not tied to something. Low speed fan? Hot Lamp?<P>Green wire to C9? Is that the hot lamp output? I can barely read this diagram.<P><p>[This message has been edited by terryk (edited 12-16-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000008||Eric_PA||12-16-2001||09:14 PM||||The hot lamp bulb is connected and functional, but I only have one fan speed output connected. The low speed fan is used and the high speed output is just a floating output right now. Also have the power steering switch tied to ground, but don't have good info for that. Not sure if it should source or sink to the ECM . (tied to ground or 12V) <P>Also the Green wire from C9 on the black connector is hooked up to the hot light. While on that subject, how do some of the pontiacs use a temp guage. Does that green wire from C9 provide other info besides off and on for a light. I want to make my temp guage work, but there is no sender on the engine, nor could I find one anywhere else in the engine compartment of a car in the yard with the same engine that had a guage. I want to know how they do that.<P>Thanks for all the help <BR>Eric<P><p>[This message has been edited by Eric_PA (edited 12-16-2001).]||||reg||14
Z||000009||terryk||12-16-2001||09:30 PM||||Ok, 'cause in 91, the 4 circuits on Quad A are the TCC controls, purge and low speed fan. <P>PS switch grounds the input to the ECM.<P>The hot lamp is seperate from the gauge. When the CTS gets to 254F (the usual value), it just pulls the ECM output low and turns on the lamp. There's nothing else going on with that circuit. GM did this because the temp switches are unreliable but the senders are pretty good. <P>There hot lamp may also be used on cars with gauges to yell at you to do something quick!<BR><p>[This message has been edited by terryk (edited 12-16-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000010||terryk||12-16-2001||09:37 PM||||There is one blurb saying the AC head pressure input is on Quad A. Strange since I'd expect that to toggle. Hmmmm...., but if the ECM sees the A/C off and the pressure switch on, it could think somethings is amiss. Or vice-versa.<P>There is one other option. Take a 1k resistor, connect one end to +12V, connect your scanner (or watch the SES) and touch the other end of the resistor to all of the unused pins of the ECM. The 1K resistor will keep any damage from occuring. You can try it connected to ground too. Maybe you can find the pin that shuts off the malf and work backwards from there.<P>TK<p>[This message has been edited by terryk (edited 12-16-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000011||Eric_PA||12-17-2001||09:44 AM||||Thanks for all the valuable information Terry. I am going to try and go through all the open inputs/outputs tonight until that SES light stops going on. I should also have my new scanner by then. So I will be in there until I find it. Then it will be back to trying to interpret the decompiled motorolla code, so I can mess with that thing. I don't know what all to look for there, but am learning a little. <P>thanks,<BR>Eric<p>[This message has been edited by Eric_PA (edited 12-17-2001).]||||reg||10
